1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:02,585
These yellow crystals are on Mars.

2
00:00:03,105 --> 00:00:04,870
Nobody staged this picture.

3
00:00:05,010 --> 00:00:09,883
A rover drove over a rock, the rock
cracked open, and this was inside.

4
00:00:10,403 --> 00:00:13,867
It is pure sulfur, the
first ever found on Mars.

5
00:00:14,387 --> 00:00:18,762
And according to the mission's own
project scientist, it shouldn't be there.

6
00:00:19,282 --> 00:00:20,637
So how did it get there?

7
00:00:20,777 --> 00:00:22,969
Scientists now have a serious answer.

8
00:00:23,109 --> 00:00:24,676
But they don't all agree on it.

9
00:00:25,196 --> 00:00:27,304
May thirtieth, twenty twenty-four.

10
00:00:27,444 --> 00:00:30,936
NASA's Curiosity rover, a car-sized robot,

11
00:00:31,076 --> 00:00:34,603
is climbing a valley on the
side of a mountain on Mars.

12
00:00:35,123 --> 00:00:37,078
The mountain is called Mount Sharp.

13
00:00:37,218 --> 00:00:42,421
It rises about three miles, five
kilometers, from the floor of Gale crater.

14
00:00:42,941 --> 00:00:47,438
Curiosity has been driving up its
lower slopes since twenty fourteen,

15
00:00:47,578 --> 00:00:50,630
reading the layers like
pages in a history book.

16
00:00:51,150 --> 00:00:53,862
The team was not looking
for sulfur that day.

17
00:00:54,002 --> 00:00:55,871
The rover was simply driving.

18
00:00:56,391 --> 00:01:01,430
One of its aluminum wheels rolled over
a pale rock, and the rock broke apart.

19
00:01:01,950 --> 00:01:03,901
There is no video of that moment.

20
00:01:04,041 --> 00:01:07,902
What exists are the photos the
rover took in the days after.

21
00:01:08,422 --> 00:01:13,455
A few days later, the camera on the
end of the robotic arm moved in close.

22
00:01:13,975 --> 00:01:17,548
Yellow crystals, hidden
under a reddish, dusty crust.

23
00:01:18,068 --> 00:01:19,954
In this view from the mast camera,

24
00:01:20,094 --> 00:01:25,601
the patch of broken pieces is about five
inches, thirteen centimeters, across.

25
00:01:26,121 --> 00:01:27,948
But yellow doesn't prove anything.

26
00:01:28,088 --> 00:01:29,798
Plenty of minerals are yellow.

27
00:01:30,318 --> 00:01:33,521
So the team used another
instrument on the same arm:

28
00:01:33,661 --> 00:01:36,655
APXS, the Alpha Particle
X-ray Spectrometer.

29
00:01:37,175 --> 00:01:40,428
It holds a small radioactive
source against the rock

30
00:01:40,568 --> 00:01:42,785
and reads the X-rays that come back.

31
00:01:42,925 --> 00:01:45,611
Every element answers
with its own signature.

32
00:01:46,131 --> 00:01:48,217
This material answered as sulfur.

33
00:01:48,357 --> 00:01:51,182
Not sulfur locked inside another mineral.

34
00:01:51,322 --> 00:01:53,390
Elemental sulfur, on its own.

35
00:01:53,910 --> 00:01:58,881
It's the same yellow substance you can
find around volcanic vents on Earth.

36
00:01:59,401 --> 00:02:01,887
Now, sulfur on Mars is not news.

37
00:02:02,407 --> 00:02:06,117
Curiosity had spent months in
a region rich in sulfates:

38
00:02:06,257 --> 00:02:11,175
minerals where sulfur is bound to
oxygen and to metals like calcium

39
00:02:11,315 --> 00:02:12,476
and magnesium.

40
00:02:12,616 --> 00:02:16,551
They are thought to have formed
as water evaporated, long ago.

41
00:02:17,071 --> 00:02:18,898
Pure sulfur is something else.

42
00:02:19,038 --> 00:02:22,334
It only forms under a
narrow range of conditions.

43
00:02:22,854 --> 00:02:27,004
On Earth, we usually find it
near volcanoes and hot springs.

44
00:02:27,524 --> 00:02:31,532
And around this part of Mount Sharp,
there is no evidence of either.

45
00:02:32,052 --> 00:02:36,772
Curiosity's project scientist,
Ashwin Vasavada, put it plainly.

46
00:02:36,912 --> 00:02:38,103
It shouldn't be there.

47
00:02:38,243 --> 00:02:40,073
So now we have to explain it.

48
00:02:40,593 --> 00:02:42,390
And it wasn't just one rock.

49
00:02:42,910 --> 00:02:44,562
Look at the floor of the channel.

50
00:02:44,702 --> 00:02:48,739
Those bright stones scattered across
it are the same kind of rock.

51
00:02:49,259 --> 00:02:52,392
Close up, their outsides
are white and full of holes.

52
00:02:52,532 --> 00:02:54,040
Inside, they are sulfur.

53
00:02:54,560 --> 00:02:56,223
Here is the strange part.

54
00:02:56,363 --> 00:02:59,005
From orbit, this patch
looked unremarkable.

55
00:02:59,525 --> 00:03:04,186
The HiRISE camera on the Mars
Reconnaissance Orbiter can spot the rover

56
00:03:04,326 --> 00:03:06,557
itself, this dark dot, from space.

57
00:03:06,697 --> 00:03:08,748
But it could not resolve the stones.

58
00:03:09,268 --> 00:03:11,584
To see them, you had to drive into them.

59
00:03:12,104 --> 00:03:14,210
The channel is called Gediz Vallis.

60
00:03:14,350 --> 00:03:18,113
It winds down the side of Mount
Sharp, and it formed late,

61
00:03:18,253 --> 00:03:20,463
when Mars was already drying out.

62
00:03:20,983 --> 00:03:24,902
Floods of water and debris,
flows of mud and boulders,

63
00:03:25,042 --> 00:03:27,852
and dry avalanches all
left their marks here.

64
00:03:28,372 --> 00:03:31,691
Water also bleached pale
halos into some of the rocks.

65
00:03:31,831 --> 00:03:34,138
One scientist on the team summed it up:

66
00:03:34,278 --> 00:03:36,792
this was not a quiet period on Mars.

67
00:03:37,312 --> 00:03:40,930
The sulfur stones were too
small and too brittle to drill.

68
00:03:41,450 --> 00:03:45,920
So engineers picked a large rock
nearby, nicknamed Mammoth Lakes,

69
00:03:46,060 --> 00:03:50,383
and found a place where the rover
could park on loose, sloping ground.

70
00:03:50,903 --> 00:03:55,718
On June eighteenth, Curiosity drilled
the forty-first sample hole of its

71
00:03:55,858 --> 00:03:56,545
mission.

72
00:03:56,685 --> 00:04:00,398
In this panorama, its arm is
still raised after drilling.

73
00:04:00,918 --> 00:04:05,064
The rock powder went into small
laboratories inside the rover.

74
00:04:05,584 --> 00:04:07,875
Curiosity spent months in this channel.

75
00:04:08,015 --> 00:04:10,597
On October eleventh, twenty twenty-four,

76
00:04:10,737 --> 00:04:14,126
it took one last look at the
sulfur field and drove on.

77
00:04:14,646 --> 00:04:16,277
The question stayed behind.

78
00:04:16,797 --> 00:04:18,499
In August twenty twenty-six,

79
00:04:18,639 --> 00:04:22,135
the team published its analysis
in the journal Science.

80
00:04:22,655 --> 00:04:23,841
Their main result:

81
00:04:23,981 --> 00:04:28,078
these stones are native sulfur, and
they appear to have formed right

82
00:04:28,218 --> 00:04:29,171
where they sit.

83
00:04:29,311 --> 00:04:31,504
They were not washed in
from somewhere else.

84
00:04:32,024 --> 00:04:36,010
The team estimates they formed
roughly three billion years ago.

85
00:04:36,530 --> 00:04:39,763
Their proposed explanation
starts deep underground.

86
00:04:39,903 --> 00:04:42,206
Magma released sulfur-rich gases.

87
00:04:42,726 --> 00:04:47,043
The gases rose and cooled in frozen
ground near the surface, and,

88
00:04:47,183 --> 00:04:50,624
likely with help from water,
turned into pure sulfur.

89
00:04:51,144 --> 00:04:55,309
Much later, as the valley
eroded, the deposit was exposed.

90
00:04:55,829 --> 00:04:58,327
That is a proposal, not a closed case.

91
00:04:58,467 --> 00:05:01,605
Another group has suggested
something quite different.

92
00:05:02,125 --> 00:05:07,760
In their idea, a meteorite impact upstream
melted a sulfur-rich layer of rock,

93
00:05:07,900 --> 00:05:12,418
and liquid sulfur flowed about four
kilometers down the valley before it

94
00:05:12,558 --> 00:05:13,329
froze.

95
00:05:13,849 --> 00:05:18,297
But that idea depends on a rock
layer that nobody has measured yet.

96
00:05:18,817 --> 00:05:20,863
One thing this discovery is not:

97
00:05:21,003 --> 00:05:22,205
evidence of life.

98
00:05:22,725 --> 00:05:26,711
The team says it sees no sign
that microbes played any role.

99
00:05:27,231 --> 00:05:30,002
What the stones do
show, the authors argue,

100
00:05:30,142 --> 00:05:33,671
is that ancient Mars had a
way to concentrate sulfur,

101
00:05:33,811 --> 00:05:36,365
driven by fluids rising from below.

102
00:05:36,885 --> 00:05:40,609
And they seem to have sat almost
untouched for billions of years.

103
00:05:40,749 --> 00:05:44,064
One of the researchers called
Mars a frozen snapshot.

104
00:05:44,584 --> 00:05:46,317
So here is where it stands.

105
00:05:46,837 --> 00:05:51,200
We know what the stones are, where they
are, and roughly how old they are.

106
00:05:51,720 --> 00:05:53,787
We don't know for sure how they got there.

107
00:05:53,927 --> 00:05:58,726
Part of the answer may sit in rock
layers upstream that Curiosity has not

108
00:05:58,866 --> 00:05:59,750
reached yet.

109
00:06:00,270 --> 00:06:04,637
Not bad, for a rock the rover only
found because it ran it over.

